  "reasoning": "Washington, DC elects a nonvoting House delegate rather than a voting representative; the Democratic nominee for 2026 is Robert White, who just won the Democratic primary and is described as the likely next delegate, indicating no sign of emerging competitiveness.[7] DC\u2019s electorate is overwhelmingly Democratic: in the 2024 primary cycle, 92% of registered voters were Democrats and only 6% were Republicans,[3] and 2025 registration estimates show about 75% Democratic vs. about 5% Republican.[12] Because there is no recent head\u2011to\u2011head general\u2011election polling for 2026, the best quantitative proxy is partisan registration and past results; these consistently show Democratic margins on the order of several dozen points, so I treat the effective baseline margin as roughly 80+ points in favor of Democrats, and the PVI as about D+10 per recent statewide figures.[1]",
